---
/** How much per week, and why the third flag sits well above 150 minutes. Rendered by /science/movement/weekly-volume; see scienceTopics.ts. */
import SplitCard from "../../components/science/SplitCard.astro";
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import VideoSlot from "../../components/VideoSlot.astro";
import {
  WEEKLY_VOLUME_SECTION,
} from "../../data/science";
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";
const mets = topicHref("mets-vs-met-h");
const zone2 = topicHref("zone-2");
const effort = topicHref("effort");
const workout = topicHref("a-workout");
const testCrf = topicHref("test-crf");

/** Which number, which ruler: the table under #two-rulers. */
const rulers: [string, string, string, string][] = [
  ["Weekly dose", "MET-minutes", "The session's one MET (the watch's energy above rest plus the 1 MET of rest; a WHOOP's figure as it is; else the Compendium; the Swain cascade for a typed heart rate) × its minutes. The session is classed by its average MET: 3.0 to 5.99 moderate, 6.0 and above vigorous.", "Logged aerobic or mixed sessions classed moderate or vigorous"],
  ["Weekly Ascent", "minutes", "The heart-rate trace, minute by minute, against your own band. Without a trace, the whole session takes its MET class.", "Every aerobic session, the walking read from your steps included"],
  ["Zone 2", "minutes", "The minutes of the trace inside your band.", "Logged aerobic or mixed sessions"],
  ["Under a workout", "minutes", "The same trace, split light / moderate / vigorous.", "That session"],
  ["Strength", "days", "No ruler of effort: two days a week is the target.", "Strength sessions"],
];
---

<SplitCard eyebrow="In the app" wide>
  <p>
    The Activity page draws your week as a climb, with flags at 150, 300
    and 610 minutes. The line is your aerobic minutes; the flags are where
    the evidence below changes what it says.
  </p>
  <svg slot="panel" viewBox="0 0 248 104" class="block h-auto w-full" aria-hidden="true">
    <line x1="0" y1="88" x2="248" y2="88" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" />
    <polygon fill="rgba(52,211,153,0.12)" points="6,86 54,72 100,58 148,40 196,26 232,18 232,86" />
    <polyline fill="none" stroke="#34d399" stroke-width="2.2" points="6,86 54,72 100,58 148,40 196,26 232,18" />
    <line x1="108" y1="88" x2="108" y2="52" stroke="#FBBF24" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
    <text x="112" y="50" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#FBBF24">150</text>
    <line x1="172" y1="88" x2="172" y2="34" stroke="#06B6D4" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
    <text x="176" y="32" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#06B6D4">300</text>
    <line x1="216" y1="88" x2="216" y2="22" stroke="#8B5CF6" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
    <text x="188" y="18" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#8B5CF6">610</text>
    <g font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="7" fill="#475569">
      <text x="2" y="100">M</text><text x="40" y="100">T</text><text x="78" y="100">W</text>
      <text x="116" y="100">T</text><text x="154" y="100">F</text><text x="192" y="100">S</text>
      <text x="228" y="100">S</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
</SplitCard>

<div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="weekly-volume" /></div>
<p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  The Weekly Ascent draws no lines to clear and has no ceiling. The
  climb rises as far as your week does, and a flag is planted where it
  passes 150, 300 and 610 measured minutes &mdash; only once passed, so
  there is never a mark ahead of you to fall short of. Someone who runs
  thirty miles every weekend and logs 1,200 minutes sees a climb to 1,200.
  The first two figures are the familiar public-health doses. The third
  is newer, larger, and measured the same way Verve measures you.
</p>
<p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">The climb is drawn as two bands.</strong> The line is
  the week&rsquo;s measured moderate-plus-vigorous minutes, exactly as before. Beneath it, the
  area is split: moderate minutes fill from the ground up, and vigorous minutes sit on top of
  them, in a darker green. Nothing is re-weighted &mdash; vigorous is not credited double the
  way WHO&rsquo;s moderate-equivalent doses are, because the axis carries measured minutes and
  one axis holds one unit. What the bands add is composition: intensity drives most of the
  fitness benefit, and a 75-minute week that was all hard running now looks like what it is
  rather than like half a target.
</p>
<h3 id="weekly-dose" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">The weekly dose, in MET-minutes</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  Minutes say how long you moved; they do not say how hard. A MET-minute does:
  one minute at a given intensity, so thirty minutes of brisk walking at 4 METs
  is 120 MET-minutes and thirty minutes of running at 10 METs is 300. The
  American College of Cardiology&rsquo;s 2026 scientific statement on prescribing
  exercise gives the weekly aerobic dose in this unit, <strong class="text-text-primary">500 to
  1,000 MET-minutes a week</strong>, the same range the 2018 Physical Activity
  Guidelines set out, with more benefit toward the top of it.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  In the app this is the <strong class="text-text-primary">Weekly dose</strong> card under the
  Ascent, and the goal you set in Settings &rarr; Profile, in MET-minutes or MET-hours as you
  prefer (1,000 MET-minutes is 16.7 MET-hours). Two rules keep the number honest. Only the
  sessions you log at moderate or vigorous effort count toward the dose; walking that Verve
  infers from your steps counts toward the minutes on the Ascent, where a step count can
  support the claim, but not toward a MET dose, where it cannot. And strength sessions are
  not aerobic dose: the guidelines give them their own target, two days a week, which the app
  counts in days. The bar fills in the Ascent&rsquo;s two greens, moderate and vigorous, so a
  week that was all hard running looks like what it was.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">The two landmarks.</strong> The bar is amber until your goal is
  reached and green from then on, and it has no top: a 1,400 week is drawn to 1,400. Nothing is
  drawn ahead of you to fall short of. Two pennants are planted only once a week has crossed
  them: at <strong class="text-text-primary">500</strong>, the floor of the guideline range, the dose
  below which the evidence for benefit thins; and at <strong class="text-text-primary">1,000</strong>,
  the top of the range, past which the extra benefit per MET-minute flattens, though it does not
  stop. A week that crosses the first has met the guideline; a week that crosses the second has
  done what the statement calls the upper end of the prescription.
</p>
<h3 id="two-rulers" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Two rulers: how a session becomes MET-minutes, and how a minute becomes moderate</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  Verve asks two different questions of the same workout and uses a different ruler for each.
  The weekly dose asks how much work the week held, in MET-minutes. The Ascent, the Zone 2 count
  and the band split under a workout ask how many minutes were spent at which effort. The first is
  a figure for the session; the second is counted minute by minute. Heart rate drives the second.
  It does not drive the first, except for a session you type in with a heart rate and no watch.
  The Monday note&rsquo;s three numbers are the three rulers at work: a week of 210 logged minutes
  can be 1,106 MET-minutes of dose with 92 of those minutes in Zone 2.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">The session&rsquo;s MET.</strong> A synced workout&rsquo;s MET comes
  from the energy the watch recorded for it: its kilocalories, your weight and the minutes, through
  the definition of a MET as 3.5 ml of oxygen per kilogram per minute, which is close to 1 kcal per
  kilogram per hour. A MET is gross: sitting is 1, and the cut-points below assume that 1 is in. A
  watch&rsquo;s <em>active</em> energy is not: Apple defines it as the energy burned above resting
  energy, and Health Connect&rsquo;s active calories exclude basal metabolic rate. So Verve adds the
  resting MET back. A 40-minute walk the watch costs at 160 kcal above rest, in a 75 kg body, is
  3.0 METs above rest and 4.0 METs in all: moderate, 160 MET-minutes. Until 1.7 the app read the
  energy as if it were the whole burn, so every watch session sat about 1 MET low, a slightly
  easier walk fell to light and counted nothing, and a week&rsquo;s dose was short by about one
  MET-minute per minute; the update corrects the stored sessions once. When the store holds no
  energy for the session, or the figure falls outside 1.5 to 18 METs and is not believable, Verve
  uses the Compendium of Physical Activities value for that activity type instead. A session you
  log by hand with a heart rate gets its MET from the heart-rate-reserve cascade (Swain 1997),
  anchored to your own <a href={testCrf}>fitness test</a> when you have one, to your age and sex
  norms when you do not; that formula returns a gross MET already. MET-minutes are then
  MET × minutes: 4.0 × 40 is 160. <a href={mets}>METs vs MET-hours</a> has the unit;
  <a href={workout}>a workout, as Verve reads it</a> has why the page prints one MET figure.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary" id="energy-with-rest">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">One writer is different: WHOOP.</strong> WHOOP writes one calorie
  figure per activity, and the resting burn is inside it. WHOOP support gives its calories as basal
  metabolic rate plus a heart-rate term (<a href="https://www.community.whoop.com/t/how-is-calorie-burn-estimated-and-how-accurate-is-it/113" target="_blank" rel="noopener">how WHOOP estimates calorie burn</a>,
  <a href="https://www.whoop.com/thelocker/calorie-tracking-science/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the science of calorie tracking</a>),
  and Cronometer, which imports WHOOP workouts, subtracts the BMR for the workout&rsquo;s duration from
  every entry for that reason (<a href="https://support.cronometer.com/hc/en-us/articles/44189146042516-WHOOP" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cronometer: WHOOP</a>).
  The founder&rsquo;s walk of 2 November 2026 is the check: 232 kcal in the WHOOP app, 232 active
  and 232 total in Apple Health, 30 minutes, 118 bpm on average. Verve reads a WHOOP figure as it is
  and adds nothing: in a 75 kg body that walk is 5.8 METs, moderate. The same 232 kcal written by an
  Apple Watch would be 6.8, because a Watch&rsquo;s 232 is the burn above rest. The rule is by
  writer and lives in one place in the code; a writer it does not know is read as the stores define
  the field, above rest.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">The session&rsquo;s class.</strong> That one average MET is read against
  fixed cut-points: below 1.5 sedentary, 1.5 to 2.99 light, 3.0 to 5.99 moderate, 6.0 and above
  vigorous. These are the absolute bands the guidelines are written in; 3 METs is brisk walking whoever
  you are. The weekly dose counts the MET-minutes of aerobic and mixed sessions whose average lands
  moderate or vigorous. A light session, a strength session and the walking Verve reads from your steps
  add nothing to the dose: the first because the guideline dose is moderate-to-vigorous by definition,
  the second because strength has its own target in days, the third because a step count cannot carry a
  MET claim.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">Minutes by effort.</strong> The band split, Zone 2 and the Ascent&rsquo;s
  moderate and vigorous minutes are counted from the heart-rate trace, minute by minute, against your own
  band: your <a href={zone2}>Zone 2 range</a>, from your resting heart rate and age or from your test. A
  minute inside the band is moderate, above it vigorous, below it light. Without a trace, a session logged
  by hand or recorded without a watch, the whole session takes the class its MET gave it. On a
  beta-blocker, where heart rate cannot be trusted, your <a href={effort}>1-to-10 effort rating</a> is the
  ruler instead.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">Why the two can disagree, and why that is right.</strong> A 60-minute
  easy run at an average of 7 METs is 420 MET-minutes of vigorous dose, because the session as a whole
  was vigorous. The same run can show 38 moderate and 22 vigorous minutes under the workout, because for
  38 of those minutes the heart sat inside your moderate band. Neither is wrong. A relative ruler, your
  heart rate against your own range, says how hard the work was for your body. An absolute ruler, METs,
  says how much work was done, in the unit the dose evidence is written in. The app keeps them apart on
  purpose, and never converts one into the other.
</p>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-well">
  <table class="w-full min-w-[720px] text-[12.5px]">
    <thead>
      <tr class="text-left font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-text-tertiary">
        <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Number</th>
        <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Unit</th>
        <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Ruler</th>
        <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Counts</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-ink/[0.06]">
      {rulers.map(([n, u, r, c]) => (
        <tr class="align-top">
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">{n}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-text-secondary">{u}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{r}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">{c}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {WEEKLY_VOLUME_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
</div>
